# The benchmark corpus is the measurement, not build output, and its jsonl is
# committed on purpose.  The blanket rule above hides a *new* fixture version
# from `git add` without saying so, which is how `plan-fixtures-v5.jsonl` came
# to be absent from the repository while its `.sha256` and manifest were
# present: the seal existed and the thing it sealed did not.  Re-including the
# directory is the only fix that does not depend on remembering `-f` each time
# a fixture version is cut.  Nothing here is re-included that was not already
# tracked -- v1..v4 are all in the index.
!finquery_rag/backend/benchmarks/**/*.jsonl

.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

.runtime/
runtime/

# `runtime/` above is meant for the deployment runtime directory at the repo
# root -- pids, status files, logs.  As written it matches a directory of that
# name at ANY depth, which silently swallowed the source packages
# `src/runtime/` and `rag_v2/runtime/`.  The cost was not theoretical:
# `src/runtime/trusted_v2_canonical_fact_store.py` was skipped, while the test
# that imports it (`tests/test_trusted_v2_canonical_fact_store.py`) was
# committed -- a tracked test that cannot run, failing the whole suite at
# collection.
#
# The negation names the *directory*: git cannot re-include a file whose parent
# directory is excluded, so `!path/to/runtime/**/*.py` does nothing at all.
!finquery_rag/backend/src/runtime/
!finquery_rag/backend/rag_v2/runtime/
#
# `tests/runtime/` is the same trap a third time: the rule swallowed the package
# directory, so a test module placed there is neither committed nor listed by
# `git status`, and its absence is invisible.  Named as a directory for the
# reason above.
!finquery_rag/backend/tests/runtime/
*.db
*.sqlite
